    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1577">

                <pb facs="1002" n="886" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg886"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 312.62/50.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to Consul Letcher.</hi><anchor
                        n="150" xml:id="d1577-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-04-14T17:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 14, 1914, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Upon request of diplomatic representative of Germany you will bring to attention
                    of Carranza reported kidnapping by Constitutionalist forces near Guaymas,
                    Sonora, of German subject named <pb facs="1003" n="887" xml:id="pg887"/> Radke
                    and informally request that he be liberated and allowed to return to his lawful
                    occupation. You will add that German representative further requests this
                    Government to ask that Constitutionalist forces give adequate protection to
                    German subjects within their jurisdiction.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bryan</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1577-f1" n="150" type="source" xml:id="d1577-f1-content">
                <p>Mutatis mutandis to the American Consul at Hermosillo. The American Chargé
                    d’Affaires at Mexico City advised of contents of the telegram April 14,
                    1914.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
